Statements:
[
    "Retriever failures are categorized into three types: coverage/completeness, relevancy, and ranking/order.",
    "Coverage/completeness refers to how much of the relevant context the retriever fetches.",
    "The metric for coverage/completeness is contextual recall.",
    "Tunable parameters for coverage/completeness include chunking hyperparameters such as chunk size and overlap, top_k, and the embedding model or embedding stage.",
    "If recall is low, increase chunk size to retrieve more information.",
    "Relevancy refers to how relevant the retrieved results are to the query.",
    "The metric for relevancy is contextual relevancy.",
    "Tunable parameters for relevancy include chunk size, other chunking hyperparameters, overlap, top_k, and the embedding model.",
    "If relevancy is low, decrease chunk size to make chunks more focused.",
    "Ranking/order refers to how well results are ordered by relevance.",
    "The metric used to study ranking is precision.",
    "Tunable parameters for ranking include the embedding model, top_k, and the use of a reranker (cross-encoder).",
    "A reranker is a separate transformer (cross-encoder) that recalculates similarities and can improve ranking.",
    "The retriever itself is a bi-encoder that returns vectors.",
    "Chunking and embedding stages are the main factors affecting retriever performance.",
    "Metrics are applied together across the application, but each targets a different component.",
    "Parameters can be tweaked per metric."
] 

